module

public import FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«211»

/-!
# Erdős Problem 211, variant: the constant 1/6 in Beck's theorem is refuted

Generated by an AI agent and machine-checked; not reviewed by a human expert.

## Statement

A line is determined by a finite point set if it contains at least two of its points. `main` is the
negation of the upstream statement `Erdos211.erdos_211.variants.one_sixth` (Formal Conjectures,
`FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/211.lean`, commit 9097e2f9, marked `category research open` there):

    ¬ ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ (n k : ℕ), N ≤ n → 1 ≤ k → k < n →
        ∀ s : Finset ℝ², s.card = n →
          (∀ L : AffineSubspace ℝ ℝ², IsLine L → (s.filter fun p => p ∈ L).card ≤ n - k) →
          (1 - ε) / 6 * (k : ℝ) * (n : ℝ) ≤ ((determinedLines s).card : ℝ)

It fails for `ε = 1/1000`. The quantitative form is `E211.counterexample`: for `R = 30030 M`,
`M ≥ 1000`, there is a set of `n ≥ 3R³` points with at most `2R²` points on any line that determines
at most `0.166 k n` lines, where `k = n - 2R²`. So the best constant in Beck's theorem is at most
`0.166 < 1/6`.

## Outline

`S` is the set of integer vectors with `-R ≤ x, y ≤ R - 1`, `1 ≤ z ≤ R - 1`, and `P` is the set of
the points `(x/z, y/z)`.

* Lemmas 1-3. Among the pairs `u, w ∈ S` that span a given determined line, take one with the
  smallest height sum `z(u) + z(w)`, with `z(u) ≥ z(w)`. If `z(u) > z(w)`, then `u - w` has left the
  box (else it could replace `u`), so `u` and `w` differ by at least `R` in `x` or in `y`. Also `u`
  and `w` are then independent modulo every prime `p` (else some `(a u + b w)/p` could replace one
  of them).
* Lemmas 4-6. Cutting the box into cubes of side `30030 = 2·3·5·7·11·13` shows that there are at
  most about `(7/16) ∏ (1 - p⁻²)(1 - p⁻³) · 8R⁶ ≤ 1.801 R⁶` such pairs with `z(u) > z(w)`. The
  product is over the six primes. There are at most `16R⁵` pairs with `z(u) = z(w)`. The sum of the
  two bounds bounds the number of lines.
* Lemmas 7-9. Distinct primitive vectors of `S` give distinct points, and a union bound over `2`,
  `3`, `5`, `7` and the odd numbers from `11` on shows that there are at least `3.298 R³ - 7R²`.
* Lemma 10. A line contains at most `2R²` points of `P`.

The numbering agrees with the written proof in `Erdos211.md`. An earlier, longer proof by the
same agents (reduced bases, volumes; not included here) gives the better constant `0.1605`.
With all primes in place of six, the present argument gives `7ζ(3)/(32ζ(2)) < 0.1599` (written proof
only, not formalized).

## Scope

* This refutes a VARIANT. Problem 211 is not resolved or changed by this file: its theorem, at least
  `c k n` lines for an absolute `c > 0`, is due to Beck and to Szemerédi and Trotter (1983) and is
  not affected; nor are the upstream statements `erdos_211`, `variants.quadratic` and
  `variants.sharpness`. What is refuted is a speculation that its author doubted.
* It concerns `k` close to `n`: with `0.166` the bound fails, for small `ε`, once `k/n` is above
  about `0.996` (with `0.1599`, not formalized, about `0.96`). The file proves nothing about smaller
  `k`; in particular the case of fixed `k`, or `k = o(n)`, is untouched. For `k ≤ 2n/3` the bound
  `k n / 6` is true, by Theorem 2.1 of de Zeeuw (below) and the count in its proof (a reviewer's
  deduction, not formalized).
* Erdős's text has `c = 1/6` without `o(1)`; the upstream form is weaker, so its negation refutes
  that one too. No defect is known in the upstream statement, and no degenerate case is used.
* With de Zeeuw's `1/9` this leaves `1/9 ≤ c ≤ 0.166` for the best constant `c`.

## Priority

New as far as known: two independent limited searches on 24-25 Nov 2026 found no public disproof of
the constant `1/6`, no published real point set with at most `o(n)` points on a line and fewer than
about `n²/6` lines, and no public Lean proof of the negation. What is claimed as likely new is the
choice of such a point set against Erdős's question and the elementary formal proof. The line count
itself is not claimed as new mathematics: the ingredients are classical, and for a close relative of
`P` it is a short corollary of known work.

* W. M. Schmidt, "The distribution of sublattices of `Z^m`", Monatsh. Math. 125 (1998), Theorem 2,
  as quoted in I. Aliev and M. Henk, https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.0234, Theorem 3.1, counts primitive
  rank-2 sublattices of `ℤ³` by shape and determinant. For the points `(x/z, y/z)` with
  `x² + y² + z² ≤ R²` it yields, after an integration, `#lines / n² → 9ζ(3)/(8π²) = 0.1370`. No one
  was found who made this deduction, and it is not used here, but a number theorist may call such
  counts known. A write-up should cite Schmidt 1998, read in the original.
* Limits of the searches: the Erdős problems site and its forum were not requested, so a comment
  there is not excluded; https://github.com/teorth/erdosproblems (data/problems.yaml) lists 211 as
  proved. Not opened: the book of Brass, Moser and Pach (ch. 7), W. Moser's problem list (Discrete
  Appl. Math. 31 (1991) 201-225), the Erdős-Purdy chapter in the Handbook of Combinatorics, Payne's
  thesis, and the papers of Schmidt and Beck. Semantic Scholar, zbMATH, the arXiv API and GitHub
  code search worked only in part; MathSciNet and Google Scholar were not used. Of the 96 works that
  OpenAlex lists as citing Borwein-Moser, all titles and only a few texts were read. These sources,
  and authors who treat such constants (F. de Zeeuw, B. Lund, M. Payne, P. Pokora, A. Dumitrescu),
  are the places to ask.
* The searches concerned the result. They predate the present, shorter argument; no separate search
  for the argument is on record.
* For an expert: is the `o(1)` of the problem meant uniformly in `k`, as upstream has it? The
  refutation uses `k/n → 1`.

## Sources and related work (none proves or refutes the statement)

* P. Erdős, "Research problems", Period. Math. Hungar. 15 (1984) 101-103, p. 103,
  https://users.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1984-18.pdf: "It would be tempting to conjecture that c = 1/6.
  That c ≤ 1/6 follows from Sylvester's result [3], perhaps the conjecture c = 1/6 is too optimistic
  and one should first look for a counter-example." The `(1+o(1))` form is credited by the wiki
  https://github.com/plasma-ai/erdos (E0211) to the Erdős problems site.
* P. Borwein and W. O. J. Moser, "A survey of Sylvester's problem and its generalizations",
  Aequationes Math. 40 (1990) 111-135, p. 124, repeat the question. They print the conjecture for
  `0 ≤ k ≤ 2`, apparently a misprint for `0 ≤ k ≤ n - 2`; for `k ≤ 2` this file says nothing.
  P. Erdős, Ann. Discrete Math. 20 (1984) 129-136, p. 132, asks for the largest `c`.
* Formal statement: https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/pull/6770. An audit,
  https://github.com/BoltonBailey/formal-conjectures-ai-review, calls it "correctly marked open".
* The bound `c k n`: J. Beck, Combinatorica 3 (1983) 281-297; E. Szemerédi and W. T. Trotter,
  Combinatorica 3 (1983) 381-392. Constants: M. S. Payne and D. R. Wood, `1/98`,
  https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.3594 (`1/93` by Payne, per de Zeeuw); F. de Zeeuw, `1/9`,
  https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08015. The latter says, after Corollary 2.2, that one cannot do better
  than `1/6`: an upper bound for `c`, as is Erdős's `c ≤ 1/6`, and consistent with `main`. Its
  example of Böröczky has about `n²/8` lines but `n/2` collinear points, so it is not an earlier
  counterexample. Its Conjecture 1.5, that the sum of `|L ∩ P|` over the determined lines is at
  least `3n²/8` if at most `n/2` points are collinear, seems not to be contradicted. This is
  numerical evidence only: the sum is `0.439 n²` for `R = 10` (exact count, with `0.169 n²` lines)
  and about `0.42 n²` for `R = 40` and `R = 80` (sampling, with about `0.16 n²` lines).
* `n²/6` three-point lines: S. A. Burr, B. Grünbaum and N. J. A. Sloane, Geom. Dedicata 2 (1974)
  397-424; Z. Füredi and I. Palásti, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 92 (1984) 561-566 (cited from upstream,
  not opened).
* Other occurrences of `1/6`, about different quantities: D. Y. B. Annor and M. S. Payne, regions of
  arrangements, https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09304; M. E. Kroeker and B. Lund, ordinary lines,
  https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21415. D. Khoshnoudirad, J. Algebra Comb. Discrete Appl. 2 (2015)
  169-190, states a bound on Farey vertices that would be far below `1/6`; it cannot hold as stated
  (Beck, Szemerédi-Trotter), and the erratum (3 (2016) 105-123) makes it conditional.
* Lean, the theorem of Problem 211 only, not used here: https://github.com/plby/lean-proofs,
  https://github.com/itsdanielyc/jsp-000195-beck. The prose of
  https://github.com/rjwalters/lean-genius/pull/29727 treats `1/6` as sharp; its Lean statements
  (Steiner triple systems) do not conflict with `main`.

